  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The pricing formula and cost benchmarks are derived from a sample of 31 operators within the Australian regulatory and geographic context, which may limit direct application to other national subsidy frameworks.",
      "what": "Using a mixed-methods dataset that combines financial audits from 31 operators with operational telemetry from 175,000 trips in Australia, this study estimates a transparent cost function for community transport. The analysis reveals a structural deficit where the volume-weighted mean cost of AUD 67.50 per trip is nearly double the current flat-rate subsidy, and exposes a rural cost paradox where low labor costs mask high capital burdens. To address this, a National Variable Pricing Formula was designed as a two-part tariff with a base flag-fall, distance rates, and premiums for remote capital and high-needs clients, which simulations show increases sector-wide cost recovery from 52% to full sustainability.",
      "why": "It provides the first empirical cost analysis of community transport, replacing arbitrary flat-rate subsidies with a transferable, activity-based pricing formula that can prevent operator insolvency without driving cost inflation."
    }
